Investigate this Wazuh alert:

{
  "severity": "critical",
  "host_observations": [
    {"host": "WS-FIN-08.corp.local", "role": "endpoint", "user": "s.chen", "first_seen": "2032-09-14T16:08:08+0000", "last_seen": "2032-09-14T16:12:00+0000"}
  ],
  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "KDC SRV discovery + PowerShell scriptblock", "technique": "T1550.003", "what_happened": "At 2032-09-14 16:08:08 UTC, AMSI EID 4104 on WS-FIN-08 captured a powershell.exe scriptblock issuing 'nslookup -type=SRV _kerberos._tcp.corp.local' AND containing the literal strings 'CreateProcessWithLogonW' + 'LOGON_NETCREDENTIALS' + a per-byte RC4 hash assembly (16 bytes hex-formatted). Rule 92217 triggered.", "chain_reason": "KDC SRV lookup + LOGON_NETCREDENTIALS P/Invoke template + literal RC4 hash bytes in the same scriptblock is the canonical Overpass-the-hash pre-flight: discover the KDC, then call CreateProcessWithLogonW with /netonly to convert a stolen NTLM hash into a usable TGT."},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Security 4624 LogonType=9 (NewCredentials)", "technique": "T1550.003", "what_happened": "At 2032-09-14 16:08:42, WS-FIN-08 Security 4624 recorded LogonType=9 (NewCredentials) for a fakeuser@corp.local principal — created by CreateProcessWithLogonW with LOGON_NETCREDENTIALS flag (0x2). The logon does NOT validate the password locally — only the outbound Kerberos call to the KDC will use it. Rule 60100 triggered.", "chain_reason": "LogonType=9 is rare in normal usage (only created by runas /netonly OR CreateProcessWithLogonW with LOGON_NETCREDENTIALS). When it appears alongside an outbound 4768 with EncType=0x17 for a previously-unseen-on-this-host account, it is the Overpass-the-hash signature."},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "AS-REQ with EncType=0x17 (RC4) immediately after", "technique": "T1558", "what_happened": "At 2032-09-14 16:08:43 (1 second after the LogonType=9), DC-CORP-01 Security 4768 (TGT requested) from source IP 10.40.55.108 (WS-FIN-08) with EncType=0x17 (RC4) for targetUserName=fakeuser. The KDC AS-REP succeeded because the OPtH-injected RC4 key matched (the attacker's stolen NTLM is the RC4-HMAC key). Rule 60102 triggered.", "chain_reason": "A 4768 AS-REQ with EncType=0x17 within seconds of a LogonType=9 from the same host = the NewCredentials session is being USED to make a Kerberos call with the attacker-provided hash. Modern Windows defaults to AES (0x12/0x13) for non-legacy accounts — RC4 here is forced by the attacker because that is the format their stolen hash is in."},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Tool-pattern literals (rubeus / mimikatz / sekurlsa::pth)", "technique": "T1550.003", "what_happened": "AMSI EID 4104 scriptblock content includes literal strings 'rubeus.exe asktgt /user:Administrator /rc4:<NTLM-hash> /domain:corp.local /ptt' AND 'mimikatz.exe sekurlsa::pth /user:Administrator /domain:corp.local /ntlm:<hash> /run:cmd.exe'. Rule 92217 fired.", "chain_reason": "Literal substrings 'sekurlsa::pth' OR 'rubeus.exe asktgt /rc4:' in a PowerShell scriptblock have no legitimate use; they are operator command templates for the public OPtH toolset."}
  ],
  "time_window": {"start": "2032-09-14T16:08:08+0000", "end": "2032-09-14T16:12:00+0000"},
  "alert_count": 3,
  "unique_steps": 4
}
